    "text": "A steel ball of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>kg</mi></math> moving with velocity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>50</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> collides with another ball of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>200</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> lying on the ground. Due to collision, the KE is lost and their internal energies change equally and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;are the temperature changes of masses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>, respectively. If the specific heat of steel is unity and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>J</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>18</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi>cal</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>, then",
